# The canonical snapshot — the certificate's one home in the tree.
#
# `DIGEST = hash(snapshot bytes)` is stated to hold by construction, never by discipline
# (crown #179, matrix.core.Snapshot). `--snapshot-at` is the surface that proves it: it
# retakes the walk at tick T into the retaining sink, hashes the bytes it kept, and asks
# whether that sha is the chain's own line for that tick. This file is the outside witness
# for the pair that
#
#     java -cp out matrix.Main --headless --ticks 600 --snapshot-at 500
#
# must print on its `SNAPSHOT tick=500` line, beside the `SNAPSHOT_MATCHES_DIGEST=true`
# verdict the same run prints. `.github/workflows/locks.yml` reads the values from here and
# from nowhere else; before #967 nothing in this repository ran the command at all, so a
# build printing `false` passed every lock the tree had.
#
# Exactly one payload line: <64 lowercase hex> <byte count> <why these values>. Blank lines
# and lines starting with `#` are ignored; a second payload line is refused rather than
# silently ignored, because a silently ignored pin is worse than no pin. The byte count is
# pinned with the sha because it is the other half of what README publishes, and because a
# seal that got smaller moves it.
#
# This pin rides with the seal: the digest walk is the walk being hashed, so every declared
# digest move (A10, D-020) rewrites this line too, in the commit that edits
# `.github/canonical-digest`. Forgetting it is red before the merge button rather than a
# stale number on the front page — which is what it was, twice, while every lock stayed
# green (#865, #961). The move history is `git log -p -- .github/canonical-snapshot`.

